module sysl.time

// Reading a clock without naming one: the two symbols a program asks for and something else defines.
//
// **`sysl.time` cannot read a clock and this file does not change that.** It declares two `extern`s
// and defines neither, which costs the module nothing -- an `extern` is a name and a signature, so
// this file asks for no capability and the calendar goes on running on a bare machine. What decides
// where the numbers come from is what the program is linked against:
//
// | linked with | what defines the symbols |
// |---|---|
// | a host program | `sysl.posix.time`, over `clock_gettime(2)` |
// | a board with an RTC chip | a package binding that chip, over whatever bus it is on |
// | a board with only a tick | the board's own package, over its timer |
//
// So the application writes `now()` and names no chip, no package and no capability, and moving the
// same program between a laptop and a board is a change to `package.hocon` rather than to any line
// of it.
//
// ## Nothing imports the supplier, which is the point and is what the compiler had to learn
//
// A consumer that had to `import` whichever module defines these would be naming the chip in its own
// source, which is the friction this exists to remove. But an `@export` is only a reachability root
// where the program reaches its module (`reference/ffi.md § @export`), so a supplier nothing imports
// was pruned -- and the program then failed at the link, naming a symbol no line of it contains.
//
// **A function supplying a symbol an `extern` the program calls has declared is a root.** That is the
// rule `reference/ffi.md § A module may supply another module's extern` states, and it is the
// accounting an archive member already gets: the definition is taken because something asked the
// linker for that name, and left out otherwise.
//
// ## One supplier per image
//
// Two definitions of a symbol is a refusal rather than a link error, because the compiler can see
// both and name them (`Exports.check`). A program gets exactly one clock, which is what it should
// get.
//
// ## The seam is decided where the DECLARING module is compiled, and that is not always the program
//
// **A host build links a prebuilt standard module, and the library was compiled whole** -- so
// `now`'s body was bound to the library's own supplier at the moment the artifact was built, and a
// package supplying these symbols in a *later* compilation cannot displace it. On a host you get the
// host clock, which is what a host program wants and is why one needs no package at all.
//
// **Where a board is served, the library has no prebuilt artifact to be bound in**: `sysl build-c`
// and every freestanding target compile `library/` from source into the program, so the declaration
// and the supplier meet in one compilation and the package's definition is the one that answers.
// That is the arrangement this exists for, and it is measured both ways rather than argued -- a
// `--lib` package supplying the seam is ignored under a prebuilt std and used under a source one.
//
// So overriding the clock is a thing a board does and not a thing a host does. A host program that
// wants a different clock is not asking for a seam; it is asking to call something else.
//
// ## Why these are `long` microseconds rather than the library's own types
//
// A seam is a **C** symbol, so what crosses it has to be a shape C can spell -- and the narrower
// reason is that whoever answers it is usually not written in sysl at all. A count of microseconds is
// the one representation an RTC driver, an SDK and a `clock_gettime` shim can all produce without
// agreeing about anything else, and the two library types are built from it here where the units are
// already known.
private extern "sysl_wall_us" c_wall_us() -> long
private extern "sysl_monotonic_us" c_mono_us() -> long

/** The wall clock: where whatever is keeping time thinks it is on the timeline.
 *
 * **Two readings of this may differ by anything at all**, including a negative amount, because it is
 * the clock a person and an `ntpd` are both allowed to set. Use it to stamp something, and use
 * `monotonic` to measure something.
 *
 * `sysl.posix.time.now` is the same reading on a host and needs no seam, so a program that is only
 * ever going to run on one is free to import that instead. What this one buys is that the same source
 * compiles against a board's clock as well.
 */
now() -> Instant = Instant(c_wall_us())

/** A monotonic counter, as a length of time from an origin this deliberately does not name.
 *
 * One reading answers nothing. **Two readings subtracted answer how long something took**, and that
 * is the only question it can be asked. It never goes backwards and nothing can set it, which is the
 * difference from `now` and the reason a measurement wants this one.
 */
monotonic() -> Duration = micros(c_mono_us())
